  "articleBody": "Scheduling inspections can feel like navigating a maze, and making a few common mistakes can really slow your project down. First off, a big no-no is having incomplete or incorrect documentation on site. Inspectors need to see approved plans and relevant paperwork, so make sure everything is readily available. Another frequent misstep is not knowing the assigned inspector or the specific contact details required for your jurisdiction; some areas in the Denver metro might have different protocols. Failing to prepare the site for the inspection is also a common issue – this means making sure there's clear access to all areas, and any necessary tools, like ladders, are on hand. Don't forget that many jurisdictions require inspections to be scheduled by a deadline the day before, so same-day requests are often not accepted, leading to delays. It’s also a mistake to assume that simply calling in an inspection means it’s automatically confirmed without a specific time or date. Always double-check and get confirmation. To avoid these headaches and keep your project moving smoothly, consider working with a pro who understands the ins and outs of local permit processes and can handle the coordination for you.",
